                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+p-brane"} super p-brane
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
          %hr/
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#Dbranes"} Boundary conditions or D-branes
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#in_terms_of_the_algebraic_data_of_the_qft_on_the_worldvolume"} In terms of the algebraic data of the QFT on the worldvolume
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#in__rational_cft"}
                        In
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi d
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
                        rational CFT
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#in__tft"}
                        In
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mi d
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
                        TFT
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#in_terms_of_geometric_data_of_the_model_background"}
                      In terms of geometric data of the
                      = succeed "-model" do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mi σ
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#fundamental_or_model_branes"}
                    Fundamental or
                    = succeed "-model" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi σ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_brane_molecule"} The Brane molecule
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#black_branes"} Black branes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#defect_branes"} Defect branes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#exotic_branes"} Exotic branes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#worldvolume_theories"} Worldvolume theories
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_superbrane_scan"} The super-brane scan
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#prehistory"} Prehistory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#boundary_conditions__dbranes"} Boundary conditions / D-branes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#fundamental_branes"} Fundamental branes
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#branes_ending_on_branes"} Branes ending on branes
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %em brane
            in formal high energy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em point particles
            to higher dimensional objects.
          %p
            The term derives from the word
            %em membrane
            that was originally used to describe 2-dimensional “particles”. When the need was felt to speak also about 3-, 4- and higher dimensional such “particles” the usage “3-brane”, “4-brane” etc. was introduced. Ordinary particles would be 0-branes in this counting, the strings in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            would be 1-branes and membranes themselves 2-branes.
          %p Generally, there are two different incarnations of branes
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  fundamental
                  = succeed "-branes" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                (as in “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"}> fundamental particle
                ”): these are given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-models"} sigma-models
                on
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
                describing propagation of single
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spacetimes"} target spacetimes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+spacetime"} target spacetime
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+sigma+models"} Green-Schwarz sigma models
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+scan"} brane scan
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+L-infinity+algebra"} super L-infinity algebra
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+cohomology"} cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black p-branes
                (as in “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"}> black hole
                ”): these are solutions to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                theories, with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+black+hole"} charged black hole
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 0
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} sources
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
                2-form, so black
                = succeed "-branes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                = succeed "-form" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
                and hence appear in those
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                theories where such exists.
          %p
            The idea is that these two concepts match where a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensate"} condensate
            of fundamental
            = succeed "-branes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-brane." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            Indeed, the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-hair+theorem"} no-hair theorem
            matches
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            (i.e. 0-branes) characterized (via the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wigner+classification"} Wigner classification
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angular+momentum"} angular momentum
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole"} black hole
            solutions of pure vacuum gravity. Accordingly, it is an old suggestion (
            %a{:href => "#EinsteinInfeldHoffmann39"}> Einstein-Infeld-Hoffmann 39
            ) that fundamental particles could be identified with singular solutions of vacuum gravity.
          %p
            This matching generalizes to higher dimensional
            = succeed "-branes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and there is an exact correspondence between fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+super+p-branes"} Green-Schwarz super p-branes
            and extremal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+solutions"} BPS
            black brane solutions.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            there is a third incarnation of branes, known as
          %ul
            %li
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
              \: these are the admissible
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
              for the 2-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+models"} sigma models
              describing
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
          %p
            One envisions that as one passes from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            version of the theory (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
            ”) these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            show
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/back-reaction"} back-reaction
            and turn into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
            seen in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+entropy"} black hole entropy
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes+in+string+theory"} black holes in string theory
          %h3#Dbranes Boundary conditions or D-branes
          %p
            Some words on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
          %h4#in_terms_of_the_algebraic_data_of_the_qft_on_the_worldvolume In terms of the algebraic data of the QFT on the worldvolume
          %p
            An abstractly defined
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "-space" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
            with certain structure (topological structure, conformal structure, Riemannian structure, etc. see
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %em open-closed QFT
            the cobordisms are allowed to have boundaries. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
            for more on this.
          %p
            In this abstract formulation of QFT a
            %strong brane
            is a type of data assigned by the QFT to boundaries of cobordisms.
          %h5#in__rational_cft
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            rational CFT
          %p
            A well understood class of examples is this one: among all 2-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            %em full rational 2d CFT
            has been understood completely, using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FFRS-formalism"} FFRS-formalism
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                (to be thought of as being the category of representations of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
                of the 2d CFT);
            %li
              %p
                a special symmetric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
                object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            In this formulation a type of
            %strong brane
            of the theory is precisely an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-brane"} bi-brane
            or
            %em defect line
            ):
          %p
            the 2d cobordisms with boundary on which the theory defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
            carry as extra structure on their connected boundary pieces a label given by an equivalence class of an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %ul
            %li
              %p triangulating the cobordism,
            %li
              %p
                labeling all internal edges by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                labelling all boundary pieces by the
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p all vertices where three internal edges meet by the multiplication operation
            %li
              %p
                and all points where an internal edge hits a moundary by the corresponding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                morphism
            %li
              %p
                and finally evaluating the resulting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            So in this abstract algebraic formulation of QFT on the worldvolume, a brane is just the datum assigned by the QFT to the boundary of a cobordism. But abstractly defined QFTs may arise from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
          %h5#in__tft
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2d
            TFT
          %p
            Another case where the branes of a QFT are under good mathematical control is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
          %p
            Particularly the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            are well understood.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the branes of the B-model (“B-branes”) form the the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (infinity,1)-category
                of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
                on the target space (often considered just in terms of its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category of an (infinity,1)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
                of quasicoherent sheaves);
            %li
              %p
                the branes of the A-model form the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} Fukaya category
                of the target space.
          %p (…)
          %h4#in_terms_of_geometric_data_of_the_model_background
            In terms of geometric data of the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            An abstractly defined QFT (as a consistent assignment of state spaces and propagators to cobordisms as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from
            %em geometric data
            \:
          %p
            Such a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
              QFT
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on a space of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to X
            from a cobordism (“worldvolume”)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to some target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that may carry further geometric data such as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
          %p
            One may therefore try to match the geometric data on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that encodes the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            that results after quantization. This gives a geometric interpretation to many of the otherwise purely abstract algebraic properties of the worldvolume QFT.
          %p
            It turns out that if one checks which geometric data corresponds to the
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em submanifolds
            of the target space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            data.
          %p
            A well-understood case is the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = G
            and the background field is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
          %p
            In this case it turns out that branes for the sigma model on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are given in the simplest case by conjugacy classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \subset G
            inside the group, and that these carry a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundle"} twisted vector bundle
            with the twist given by the Kalb-Ramond background bundle. These vector bundles are known in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            literature as
            %em Chan-Paton vector bundles
            \. The geometric intuition is that a QFT with certain boundary condition comes from a quantization of spaces of maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to G
            that are restricted to take the boundary of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to these submanifolds.
          %p
            More generally, one finds that the geometric data that corresponds to the branes in the algebraically defined 2d QFT is given by cocycles in the twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            The case of rational 2d CFT considered so far is only the best understood of a long sequence of other examples. Here the collection of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            – identified with the collection of all internal modules over an internal frobenius algebra, forms an ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            More generally, at least for 2-dimensional
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
            of boundary condition objects. For instance for what is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            2-d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            on some Calabi-Yau space.
          %p
            Starting with Kontsevich’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            reformulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
            the study of (derived) D-brane categories has become a field in its own right in pure mathematics.
          %p … lots of further things to say …
          %h3#fundamental_or_model_branes
            Fundamental or
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            one speaks apart from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            also about
            %strong fundamental branes
            \. These are the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            in the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            themselves.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
                this describes the ordinary quantum mechanics of a point particles on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %em fundamental particles
                for instance of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
                this describes the quantum propagation of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
                %em fundamental string
                or F1-brane (fundamental 1-brane).
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
                this describes the quantum propagation of a membrane.
            %li
              %p
                There are good indications that there is a way to describe heterotic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
                not in terms of fundamental 1-branes but in terms of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                of a fundamental 5-brane – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic dual
                of the 1-brane in 10-dimensions.
            %li
              %p etc.
